sequenceDiagram
  %% One Ansible phase, conn-server-rollout.yml, visits the admitted Conn Server Nodes one at a time, in order. docs/reference/development-containers.md#conn-server-rollout.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · POST /api/v1/fleet/conn-server/rollout
  %% kind: fleet.conn_server_rollout
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  Note over GW: Read the pin and<br/>the Conn Server Nodes
  %%> When:
  %%> - an operator posts `{"dry_run", "scope"}` to `POST /api/v1/fleet/conn-server/rollout`; both are required, so no default reaches the whole fleet
  %%> - scope `nodes` also takes `node_ids`, one to four ids that each have a record, or a validation error; `node_ids` with another scope is refused
  %%> - the release fleet pass admits the reviewed dry run and canary update only if one Node is the canary and its Tool desires that exact release; otherwise it leaves Conn alone
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - resolve the exact host release from the active file catalog or the Node's desired version; an override cannot change managed intent
  %%> - exactly one `conn_server_nodes` record is the canary, or `conn_server.canary_unset`
  %%> - a gated promotion requires a passing canary operation ID, newer than the latest canary check, which passed, and a checked dry run for every remaining Node
  %%> Picks:
  %%> - `canary`: one Node; `fleet`: enrolled, nonwithdrawn followers on the reviewed version, otherwise canary first; `nodes`: named records in request order
  %%> - each record names platform, path, service, port, user and whether to notify before the restart; Nodes without one and container Conn Servers are out of scope
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>conn-server-rollout
  %%> Input:
  %%> - version, dry run, artifacts and signing identity, notice, and pinned SSH targets; withdrawal names one withdrawn Linux service and needs no replacement asset
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - at most four Nodes, so the 13,620 s deadline covers each Node's worst case and its rollback, or `conn_server.too_many_nodes`
  loop each Node, in the admitted order
    SW->>N: Download and check<br/>conn-server-rollout
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - Mac: `launchctl print gui/<uid>/<label>` checks the configured LaunchAgent before staging, including in a dry run, or `conn_server.service_not_loaded`
    %%> - checks reachability, then downloads the selected asset into staging and checks its SHA-256, or `conn_server.hash_mismatch`
    %%> - Mac: `codesign --verify --strict --deep`, the selected release's `TeamIdentifier` and bundle id, or `conn_server.signature_invalid`, `team_mismatch`, `bundle_mismatch`
    %%> - Linux: the unit's `ExecStart` is `<current>/conn-server` and the tarball holds `conn-server-<X>-linux-x64/conn-server`, or `conn_server.layout_mismatch`
    %%> - Linux: `/health` on the unit's `--host`, or 127.0.0.1 when it has none or a wildcard, answers 200 with `server: conn-server` and a version, or `conn_server.health_unreachable`
    %%> - Mac: a direct `/health` read on 127.0.0.1 with proxies and redirects disabled requires HTTP 200, `server: conn-server` and a nonblank version string, or `conn_server.health_unreachable`
    alt Mac: staged `codesign -dr -` differs from the installed bundle
      Note over N: Hold, install nothing<br/>held
      %%> Why:
      %%> - a new designated requirement resets the TCC grants; a hold stops a gated promotion or a canary update; earlier pins hold only the affected follower
    else dry run
      Note over N: Stop after the checks<br/>checked
    else checks pass
      opt the record asks for a notice
        SW->>SW: Send the notice<br/>conn-server-notice
        %%> What it does:
        %%> - posts or runs `orbit.conn.server_rollout_notice` before anything changes; a failed notice fails the Node (`conn_server.notice_failed`)
        %%> - logs neither the URL, the command nor their output
      end
      SW->>N: Back up and swap<br/>on the same path
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - Mac: a new backup folder (`conn_server.backup_exists` when it exists), the installed bundle into it, the staged bundle onto the same path, so TCC grants stay
      %%> - Linux: a new `releases/<X>-<run>` folder, then `current` moves to it with one rename
      SW->>N: Restart<br/>kickstart or systemctl
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - Mac: `launchctl kickstart -k gui/<uid>/<label>`, never a start from the SSH session; Linux: restarts the unit
      %%> - never reads or prints a LaunchAgent plist; every command has a task timeout
      loop each round, up to 180 s
        opt Mac: a UserNotificationCenter prompt is pending
          SW->>N: Answer the prompt<br/>System Events
          %%> What it does:
          %%> - reads the prompt's static texts and clicks Allow only on a Conn Server prompt for an expected permission
          %%> - any other prompt fails the Node (`conn_server.unexpected_prompt`)
        end
        SW->>N: GET /health<br/>on 127.0.0.1 or the unit's --host
        %%> What it does:
        %%> - waits for `server: conn-server` and the pin's exact version
        %%> - Mac: uses the same direct probe and response checks as the preflight read
      end
      alt /health names the version and the canary thread read succeeds
        opt release canary
          SW->>N: Read the known thread<br/>authenticated Conn CLI
        end
        Note over N: Remove the backup<br/>updated
      else the swap, restart, prompt or health fails
        SW->>N: Roll back<br/>restore and restart
        %%> What it does:
        %%> - Mac: sets the new bundle aside, puts the backup back on the path and kickstarts again; Linux: points `current` back and restarts
        %%> - the Node fails with its cause, `conn_server.health_check_failed` when `/health` never names the version; `rolled_back` is true when health confirms the preflight version
      end
    end
  end
  Note over SW: Write each Node's result<br/>result
  alt no failed Node or blocking hold
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Operation result lists each Node's outcome: `updated`, `checked` or `held`
  else a Node fails or a hold stops the run
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>NODE · error_code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - `error_code` is `conn_server.node_failed`, `conn_server.canary_held` for a held canary, or `conn_server.node_held` during promotion
    %%> Why stop:
    %%> - later Nodes are not visited and report `not_reached`, so one bad release reaches at most one Node
  end
